    'content' => <<<'HTML'
<p>You can be warm, funny and genuinely lovely and still get very few messages if your profile is working against you. The good news is that most dating profile mistakes are small, and each one can be fixed in a few minutes. You do not need to become a different person. You only need to remove the things that quietly put people off.</p>
<p>Below are the mistakes we see most often, grouped by photos, words and safety, with a simple fix for each. Read them with a kind eye, because nearly everyone has made at least one.</p>

<h2>Dating profile mistakes with photos</h2>
<p>Photos are the first thing anyone sees, so mistakes here cost the most. The most common ones are:</p>
<ul>
<li>Only one photo, or none at all. People are hesitant to message a profile without a face.</li>
<li>Your face hidden by sunglasses, a cap, a mask or a filter in every picture.</li>
<li>Blurry, dark or far-away pictures where nobody can really see you.</li>
<li>Group photos as the main picture, so viewers cannot tell which person you are.</li>
<li>Pictures that are many years old, which cause awkwardness at the first meeting.</li>
</ul>
<p>The fix is simple. Use three or four clear, recent photos in good natural light, with your face visible in the first one. Our guide to the <a href="/blog/best-dating-profile-photos">best dating profile photos</a> goes through lighting, smiles and what to leave out.</p>

<h2>Bio mistakes that make people scroll past</h2>
<p>The words on your profile should sound like a warm person you would like to meet. These slips do the opposite:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Leaving it blank.</strong> It suggests you are not serious and gives no conversation starter.</li>
<li><strong>Writing "ask me".</strong> People want a reason to ask, not a job to do.</li>
<li><strong>Using clichés.</strong> "I love to laugh, travel and have fun" describes almost everyone.</li>
<li><strong>Listing complaints.</strong> "No drama, no liars, no time wasters" sounds like a person who has been hurt and is bracing for more.</li>
<li><strong>Writing a novel.</strong> A very long bio is tiring to read, and most people will skip it.</li>
</ul>
<p>Instead, write three to six short sentences with specific details, such as a favourite place, a hobby or a goal. Add one honest line about what you are looking for and finish with a question. Our guide on <a href="/blog/how-to-write-a-dating-profile-bio">how to write a dating profile bio</a> has a formula and sample bios you can copy.</p>

<h2>Being too negative or too demanding</h2>
<p>It is fair to have standards. The trouble starts when your profile is mostly about what you will not accept. A long list of demands can make you seem rigid, and it filters out good people who simply phrase things differently.</p>
<p>Turn each negative into a positive. Instead of "no liars", write "I value honesty". Instead of "must be tall, rich and religious", pick the one or two things that truly matter and describe the kind of relationship you would enjoy. Positive language invites people in, while negative language sets up a wall before the conversation has begun.</p>

<h2>Dishonest details that backfire</h2>
<p>It can be tempting to nudge the truth: a younger age, a better job, a different relationship status or a picture that is a little old. These shortcuts always catch up with you. If someone meets you and finds the profile was not true, trust is gone before you have had a chance to impress them in person.</p>
<p>Honesty does not mean sharing everything. It means that what you do share is true. You can keep your salary private and still be honest about your work. You can leave out the details of a painful past and still be truthful about where you are now. Real details attract people who want the real you, and those are the people worth meeting.</p>

<h2>Giving away too much personal information</h2>
<p>Some of the most serious dating profile mistakes are about safety rather than style. Do not put your phone number, social media handles, home address, workplace name or daily routine on your profile. Avoid photos that show your gate, your plate number or your office badge.</p>
<p>It is far safer to chat on the platform first and share more once you trust someone. Our guide to <a href="/blog/protect-your-privacy-on-dating-apps">protecting your privacy on dating apps</a> explains what to hold back and when. Also remember that anyone who pushes you for money or private details early on is a warning sign, which you can read about in <a href="/blog/romance-scams-in-kenya-warning-signs">romance scams in Kenya</a>.</p>

<h2>Forgetting that a profile is a conversation</h2>
<p>A profile is not a monologue. It should leave the door open for people to say hello. Many profiles fail because there is nothing to respond to: no named hobbies, no question, no quirky detail. Reading them feels like reading a form.</p>
<p>Add a hook. Mention something you are curious about, a food you are trying to master, a place you want to visit or a debate you will happily have. "Settle this for me: is the best chapati at home or at a roadside stall?" is far easier to answer than "Hi, I am looking for someone special."</p>

<h2>Messaging habits that undo a good profile</h2>
<p>Even a lovely profile can be let down by what happens after the first hello. A few habits are worth watching:</p>
<ul>
<li>Sending the same copied message to everyone, which people can spot at once.</li>
<li>Asking for a phone number or WhatsApp in your first message.</li>
<li>Replying only with one word, which leaves the other person to carry the chat.</li>
<li>Sending many messages in a row when you have had no reply.</li>
<li>Getting upset or rude when someone is not interested.</li>
</ul>
<p>Treat every chat as a conversation between two real people. Ask about something they mentioned, share something about yourself and give them space to answer. If someone says no or goes quiet, thank them and move on. Our guide to <a href="/blog/how-to-handle-rejection-gracefully">handling rejection gracefully</a> can help when it stings.</p>

<h2>Letting your profile go stale</h2>
<p>A profile that has not changed for a year can look forgotten. Your life moves, and your profile should keep up. Update your photos when your look changes, refresh your bio when your goals shift and check that your interests still match what you enjoy.</p>
<p>Make it a habit to review your profile every month or two. Look at it as a stranger would, and ask: would I message this person? If not, tweak one thing. Small, steady changes are easier than a big overhaul, and they keep your profile feeling alive.</p>

<h2>A quick checklist before you save</h2>
<p>Mistakes are normal, and fixing them is easy. Do not delete your whole profile because one part is weak. Change the photo, tighten the opening line or remove the private detail, and give it a week to see how the replies change.</p>
<p>Before you log off, run through these questions:</p>
<ol>
<li>Is my first photo a clear, recent picture of my face?</li>
<li>Does my bio include at least two specific details?</li>
<li>Have I said what I am looking for in one kind sentence?</li>
<li>Is everything I wrote true?</li>
<li>Have I kept my number, address and workplace private?</li>
<li>Does my profile end with something easy to reply to?</li>
</ol>
<p>If you can answer yes to all six, you are in good shape. Consider adding a verified badge for extra trust, which we explain in <a href="/blog/why-profile-verification-matters">why profile verification matters</a>. Then <a href="/browse">browse people near you</a> and send a message, or <a href="/register">create your free profile</a> and start fresh.</p>
